{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgmgrkzLY3dDj9H2wwTyP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No structure‑ or ΔΔG‑computing tool is available to me in this session (no shell, no PDB/FoldX/Rosetta access), so the following is from my own knowledge of the ubiquitin fold and general protein‑stability principles — not from a calculation I ran. I have flagged what is a lookup, what is a rule of thumb, and what I am genuinely unsure about.\n\n## The structural logic\n\nUbiquitin is a 76‑residue β‑grasp fold (canonical structure: PDB **1UBQ**, Vijay‑Kumar et al. 1987). All three positions are hydrophobic, but they sit in very different environments:\n\n| Mutation | Location | Burial | Volume lost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **V26A** | middle of the α‑helix (res. ~23–34), side chain packing onto the β‑sheet | essentially completely buried — V26 is usually described as the most deeply buried residue in ubiquitin | 2 methyls (~50 Å³) |\n| **L67A** | β5 strand (C‑terminal strand, res. ~66–72), side chain pointing into the core | buried core residue | 3 carbons (~75 Å³) |\n| **I44A** | β‑sheet surface; the central residue of the **L8/I44/V70/H68 hydrophobic patch** | only partially buried — this patch is solvent‑exposed by design, it is the docking site for UBA/UIM/CUE domains and the proteasome | 3 carbons, but much of it was already solvent‑exposed |\n\nI am describing these burial states from recollection of the 1UBQ structure; I did not compute solvent‑accessible surface area here.\n\n## Expected consequences\n\n- **V26A and L67A** both remove side‑chain atoms from a fully packed core and create a cavity. The standard empirical scaling for cavity‑creating mutations at fully buried sites (Matthews' work on T4 lysozyme and similar systems) is roughly **~1.2–1.5 kcal/mol per buried methylene/methyl group removed**, giving ~2 kcal/mol for Val→Ala and ~3 kcal/mol for Leu→Ala at fully buried positions. Both should therefore be **strongly destabilising**. V26A gets no help from helix propensity, since Ala is a good helix former — the cost is almost entirely packing.\n- **I44A** should be only **mildly destabilising** (order of ~1 kcal/mol), because the side chain was partly solvent‑exposed to begin with. Its dramatic effect is *functional*, not thermodynamic: I44A is the textbook \"hydrophobic‑patch knockout\" that abolishes recognition by most ubiquitin‑binding domains and by the proteasome.\n\n## Ranking, and my confidence in it\n\nOn buried volume alone, **L67A > V26A >> I44A**. But the margin between L67A and V26A is small and I would not bet heavily on the order: V26 is the more completely buried of the two and sits at the helix–sheet interface, so measured values could easily put V26A first.\n\nQuantitative ΔΔG values for exactly these substitutions do exist in the literature — the Jackson lab's Φ‑value analysis of ubiquitin folding (Went & Jackson, *Protein Eng Des Sel* 2005) scanned a large panel of core mutants including positions 26, 44 and 67. I cannot retrieve that table with a tool here and I will not quote numbers from it from memory, as I would risk inventing them. If you want the experimental ordering rather than the structural prediction, that paper is the place to check.\n\nConfidence: **high** that V26A and L67A are substantially destabilising and I44A is comparatively minor; **low** on the specific L67A‑vs‑V26A ordering.\n\n## ANSWER\n\n- **V26A — strongly destabilising** (completely buried core residue at the helix–sheet interface; ~2 kcal/mol expected).\n- **L67A — strongly destabilising** (buried core residue in β5; ~3 kcal/mol expected).\n- **I44A — only mildly destabilising** (~1 kcal/mol); it sits in the solvent‑exposed L8/I44/V70/H68 patch, so it cripples *binding* to ubiquitin receptors and the proteasome rather than folding stability.\n\n**Worst: L67A**, by largest buried volume removed — but V26A is close enough that the order is uncertain, and V26 is the more deeply buried site. These are structure‑based predictions, not values from a tool run; no ΔΔG calculator was available in this session."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18929,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18929},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"32828c08-5a9b-4a86-a924-d09cb81011d0","uuid":"5389c204-625e-43f5-a86a-42948d2cf42b","timestamp":"2026-11-06T12:39:41.637Z","request_id":"req_011CgmgrkngBeBdyUgvXfeQp"}
